FerrionOS change to relibc (MIT license, kept), made against upstream commit 74b5e7e315043a21b599f271d5692727aa54f64d with the patches listed before it in recipes/core/relibc/recipe.toml applied. In the crate redox-ioctl, `define_ioctl_data!` no longer needs a payload or its arrays to be aligned, and sends padding as zeros:
- A scheme's view of a payload copies the fixed fields out with an unaligned read and, when a setter changed one, writes them back field by field as the view is dropped.
- An array whose bytes are aligned for its element type is used in place; one that is not is copied out and, when it was written, written back as the view is dropped.
- So a view writes nothing that the scheme did not change: a payload sent with a write-only call reaches the scheme read-only.
- An array's length is computed with an overflow check; an overflow gets EINVAL.
- The caller's side writes the fixed fields one by one into zeroed bytes, and reads them back with an unaligned read.
- A view gains `<array>_mut()`, which writes an array in place and leaves its count as the caller sent it, beside `set_<array>()`, which also sets the count to the data's length.
The wire format does not change, and the view's existing methods keep their signatures. Unit tests cover an array out of alignment, a payload at an odd address, padding, an array written in place, a payload in read-only memory, and a count beyond the payload. The display drivers in base build against this crate through recipes/core/base/ferrion-redox-ioctl-path.patch.

